<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-depreciation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Depreciation</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.4 Commercial Arithmetic III
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Compute appreciation and depreciation in value of assets
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Depreciation: reduction in value of assets over time.
         Suggested activity: Learners calculate the value of a motorbike or a school laptop after several years of depreciation.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Value of an asset over time with a slider for the depreciation rate, plotted as a decay curve. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLO: Essential 2.4 (c), depreciation (the skeleton's SLO line, shared with 14.3, is kept). One lesson, flat anatomy. Reducing balance throughout.
         Activity: as planning notes (a boda boda motorbike losing value year by year; a school laptop in the first example)
         JSXGraph: fig-depreciation-curve (assets/jsxgraph/depreciation-curve.js), as planning notes
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/14-commercial-arithmetic-iii/04-depreciation/ -->

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> depreciation, the rate of depreciation and the reducing-balance method; the yearly losses shrink.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find a value after <m>n</m> years and the depreciation in Ksh; handle a different rate in the first year; find a rate from two values.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> motorbikes, laptops, matatus and tractors.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners work out a motorbike's value year by year and test the claim that it loses the same amount every year.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Start with the friend's claim in part (b) and let groups test it before they work out the table.</p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-a-boda-boda-motorbike">
        <title>A Boda Boda Motorbike</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Otieno buys a motorbike for his boda boda business for Ksh <m>160\,000</m>. Each year it loses <m>15\%</m> of its value at the start of that year.</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Find its value after <m>1</m>, <m>2</m> and <m>3</m> years.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>160\,000 - 24\,000 = 136\,000</m>; <m>136\,000 - 20\,400 = 115\,600</m>; <m>115\,600 - 17\,340 = 98\,260</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Otieno's friend says: <q><m>15\%</m> of <m>160\,000</m> is <m>24\,000</m>, so after <m>3</m> years it is worth <m>160\,000 - 3 \times 24\,000 = 88\,000</m>.</q> Who is right?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Otieno's table. The friend took <m>15\%</m> of the original price every year, but each year's loss is <m>15\%</m> of a smaller value, so the losses shrink: <m>24\,000</m>, <m>20\,400</m>, <m>17\,340</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>What single number multiplies the value each year?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>0.85</m>, since <m>100\% - 15\% = 85\%</m>. Check: <m>160\,000 \times 0.85^3 = 98\,260</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To name depreciation and its formula from the activity.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (c) found the multiplier <m>0.85 = 1 - \dfrac{15}{100}</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>The multiplier for a rate of depreciation of <m>r\%</m> is <m>1 - \dfrac{r}{100}</m>; the losses get smaller each year.</p></li>
                <li><p>Depreciation is the price minus the value, <m>P - V</m>.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-depreciation">
        <title>Depreciation</title>
        <p>An asset <term>depreciates</term> when its value falls over time; the fall in value is its <term>depreciation</term>. In the <term>reducing balance</term> method, each year's loss is a percentage, the <term>rate of depreciation</term> <m>r</m>, of what the item is worth at the start of that year, so the losses get smaller. An item bought for <m>P</m> is worth, after <m>n</m> years,
            <md>V = P\left(1 - \frac{r}{100}\right)^n</md>
            and the depreciation is <m>P - V</m>.</p>
    </insight>

    <note xml:id="note-straight-line-depreciation">
        <title>Another Way to Depreciate</title>
        <p>Some businesses take the same amount off an item's value every year; this is called <q>straight-line</q> depreciation. In this chapter, depreciation always means the reducing-balance method.</p>
    </note>

    <figure xml:id="fig-depreciation-curve">
        <caption>The value of an item that depreciates at a fixed yearly rate, by reducing balance.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-depreciation-curve" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/depreciation-curve.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-depreciation-curve" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Drag <alert>P</alert> to set the price and <alert>r</alert> to set the rate of depreciation per cent per year. The solid curve is <m>V = P\left(1 - \dfrac{r}{100}\right)^t</m>; the dots mark the end of each year.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Drag the year marker <alert>n</alert> to read the value after <m>n</m> years. The dotted line marks half the price.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Tick <alert>same amount each year</alert> to add a dashed line that loses <m>r\%</m> of the price every year. With <m>P = 160\,000</m>, <m>r = 15</m> and <m>n = 3</m> the curve gives <m>98\,260</m> and the line <m>88\,000</m>, as in <xref ref="act-a-boda-boda-motorbike"/>.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-a-school-laptop">
        <title>A School Laptop</title>
        <statement>
            <p>A school buys a laptop for Ksh <m>65\,000</m>. It depreciates at <m>20\%</m> a year. Find its value after <m>3</m> years and the depreciation.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The multiplier is <m>1 - 0.2 = 0.8</m>.
                <md>V = 65\,000 \times 0.8^3 = 65\,000 \times 0.512 = 33\,280</md>
                The value is Ksh <m>33\,280</m> and the depreciation is <m>65\,000 - 33\,280 = 31\,720</m>, Ksh <m>31\,720</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-a-matatus-first-year">
        <title>A Matatu's First Year</title>
        <statement>
            <p>A new matatu costs Ksh <m>4\,800\,000</m>. It loses <m>20\%</m> of its value in the first year and <m>10\%</m> a year after that, each time on its value at the start of the year. Find its value after <m>5</m> years.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Multiply by <m>0.8</m> for the first year and by <m>0.9</m> for each of the next four years:
                <md>4\,800\,000 \times 0.8 \times 0.9^4 = 3\,840\,000 \times 0.6561 = 2\,519\,424</md>
                The value is Ksh <m>2\,519\,424</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-finding-the-rate-of-depreciation">
        <title>Finding the Rate of Depreciation</title>
        <statement>
            <p>A tailor's sewing machine worth Ksh <m>72\,000</m> is worth Ksh <m>61\,200</m> a year later. Find the rate of depreciation.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The loss is <m>72\,000 - 61\,200 = 10\,800</m>, so the rate is
                <md>\frac{10\,800}{72\,000} \times 100 = 15\%</md></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

    <p>Type amounts as plain numbers, without Ksh, spaces or commas, for example <c>45326</c>. Give money to the nearest shilling and percentages to 1 decimal place unless the question says otherwise; exact answers in cents are also accepted.</p>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-value-after-depreciation">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-value-after-depreciation" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/14-commercial-arithmetic-iii/04-depreciation/Value-after-depreciation.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-adding-percentages-claim">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-adding-percentages-claim" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/14-commercial-arithmetic-iii/04-depreciation/Adding-percentages-claim.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-choose-a-rate-of-depreciation">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-choose-a-rate-of-depreciation" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/14-commercial-arithmetic-iii/04-depreciation/Choose-a-rate-of-depreciation.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-depreciation">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-tractor-depreciates-11">
            <statement><p>A tractor bought for Ksh <m>2\,400\,000</m> depreciates at <m>11\%</m> a year. Find its value after <m>4</m> years, to the nearest shilling.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>Ksh <m>1\,505\,814</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-phone-depreciates-30">
            <statement><p>A phone bought for Ksh <m>28\,000</m> loses <m>30\%</m> of its value each year. Find its value after <m>2</m> years and the depreciation.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>Value Ksh <m>13\,720</m>; depreciation Ksh <m>14\,280</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-percentage-lost-in-five-years">
            <statement><p>An item loses <m>10\%</m> of its value each year, by reducing balance. What percentage of its value has it lost after <m>5</m> years?</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>41.0\%</m> (not <m>50\%</m>)</p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>0.9^5 = 0.59049</m>, so the item keeps <m>59.049\%</m> of its value and has lost <m>40.951\%</m>, about <m>41.0\%</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-computer-two-rates">
            <statement><p>A cyber caf&#xe9;'s computer costs Ksh <m>95\,000</m>. It loses <m>22\%</m> of its value in the first year and <m>15\%</m> a year after that. Find its value after <m>3</m> years, to the nearest shilling.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>Ksh <m>53\,537</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>95\,000 \times 0.78 \times 0.85^2 = 53\,537.25</m>, so Ksh <m>53\,537</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-rate-of-depreciation-posho-mill">
            <statement><p>A posho mill worth Ksh <m>340\,000</m> is worth Ksh <m>272\,000</m> a year later. Find the rate of depreciation.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>20\%</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-below-half">
            <title>Challenge: Below Half</title>
            <statement><p>A motorbike depreciates at <m>15\%</m> a year. After how many whole years is it first worth less than half its price?</p></statement>
            <hint><p>Find the first power of <m>0.85</m> that is less than <m>0.5</m>.</p></hint>
            <answer><p><m>5</m> years</p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>0.85^4 = 0.522</m>, more than half; <m>0.85^5 = 0.444</m>, less than half. So after <m>5</m> years, whatever the price.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-depreciation">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Work through the activity and <xref ref="example-a-school-laptop"/>. Give a card: <q>multiply by <m>1 - \dfrac{r}{100}</m> each year</q>. Assign <xref ref="ex-tractor-depreciates-11"/> and <xref ref="ex-phone-depreciates-30"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-tractor-depreciates-11"/> to <xref ref="ex-rate-of-depreciation-posho-mill"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-below-half"/>. Extension: can a value ever reach zero by reducing balance? (No: it is multiplied by a positive number each year, so it gets ever smaller but never reaches zero.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

    <p component="lesson-plan">Closing question for the class: why might a boda boda rider want to know how fast a motorbike loses value before buying one?</p>

